{% set local_problem %}
<p>Downspouts are often overlooked, but on Arab's hilly terrain, they're critical to protecting your home. When your downspout deposits water, it needs to flow away from your foundation — not toward it. On a slope, water that starts at your foundation gains velocity as it flows downhill (about 1.5x faster on a 10% grade), concentrating erosion and increasing hydrostatic pressure against your foundation walls.</p>

<p>We see Arab homes all the time with downspouts that were clearly installed without considering the terrain. Water dumps at the foundation's uphill side, flows down around the house, and collects at the low point — often right where a basement or crawl space entrance is located.</p>

<p>Other common problems: too few downspouts for gutter length (50-60+ feet apart when maximum should be 35-40), undersized 2x3 downspouts that overflow during heavy rain, missing or damaged extensions that let water pool at the foundation, and downspouts positioned where they create drainage problems for neighbors downhill.</p>

<p><strong>Our opinion:</strong> Before you spend $2,000+ on new gutters because of overflow, let us look at your downspouts. Half the overflow problems we see in Arab are downspout issues — not enough of them, wrong size, bad positioning. That's a much cheaper fix than replacing the entire system.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Proper downspout installation on hillside properties requires planning that goes beyond just connecting a pipe to your gutter. We consider:</p>

<p><strong>Capacity:</strong> One downspout for every 35-40 linear feet of gutter run, maximum. And they should be 3x4 inch, not 2x3. The larger size handles roughly twice the flow rate. During intense storms, that difference determines whether water drains away or backs up and overflows.</p>

<p><strong>Terrain Analysis:</strong> We assess your lot's slope and identify the natural direction water wants to flow. Then we position downspouts to work with gravity, not against it. On hillside lots, this often means downspouts on the downhill side of the house, routing water away from the foundation toward lower ground.</p>

<p><strong>Extension Strategy:</strong> On sloped lots, downspouts often need to deposit water 6-10 feet from the foundation — sometimes more. We configure extensions or underground connections to achieve safe discharge distances.</p>

<p><strong>Warning from experience:</strong> Those corrugated black flex pipes people bury in their yards? They clog within 2-3 years. Every time. The corrugations trap debris, roots grow into them, and water stops flowing. If you want buried drainage, use smooth PVC. We dig up failed flex pipe constantly.</p>
{% endset %}

